WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
58 well-blow samples just produce full genomes, sparing 26 humpbacks any biopsy. This is Planetarian Daily Episode 247. Bacterial communities inside scorpion venom, a plasma protein panel for insulin resistance, and a field test of rock-weathering follow, with a science deep dive on those venom microbes. University of St. Andrews researchers working with the University of Copenhagen, the North Coast Citation Society, and fisheries and oceans Canada recovered as detailed by Fizz.org.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.580
Borg, both nuclear and mitochondrial genomes from the exhaled breath of North Pacific humpbacks.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:40.300
About 84% of the genetic material in a given sample belong to the whale.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:46.340
Airway microbes rode along in those same collections and accounted for roughly 16% of the DNA.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:52.000
When the same whales had been biopsied, those biopsy sequences agreed with the blow-derived

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:52.580
genomes.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:56.940
Blow-taken more than once across two years gave the same genetic picture.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:02.080
Here, diversity estimates lined up with the biopsy numbers, and the populations showed

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.400
little recent inbreeding.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:07.480
Three previously undescribed mitochondrial variants appeared in the set.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:13.280
Every collection ran under Gitgaat First Nation research agreements, and a fisheries and oceans

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:14.200
Canada permit.

00:01:15.265 --> 00:01:20.405
In British Columbia's Kitimat Fjord system, sterile Petri dishes flew on commercial drones

00:01:20.405 --> 00:01:22.165
through the breath of individual whales.

00:01:22.165 --> 00:01:27.705
Those dishes returned low-coverage nuclear genomes and high-coverage mitochondrial genomes,

00:01:28.145 --> 00:01:31.165
gathering host DNA and airway microbes in one pass.

00:01:31.825 --> 00:01:34.725
More than one-third of the flights launched directly from land.

00:01:35.205 --> 00:01:40.705
The resulting data set is the first population-level whole genome resource for north-Pacific humpbacks.

00:01:41.598 --> 00:01:46.878
Novel sequencing of Scorpion Venom at Eastern Connecticut State University identified in

00:01:46.878 --> 00:01:51.578
a Hartford-Coron report on a study titled Microbiota Discovered in Scorpion Venom.

00:01:51.578 --> 00:01:56.598
previously unknown microbial communities. Venom from multiple species showed

00:01:56.598 --> 00:02:00.558
bacterial diversity that changed from animal to animal and from species to

00:02:00.558 --> 00:02:05.038
species. A distinctive group called scorpion group 1 turned up in the eastern

00:02:05.038 --> 00:02:09.798
swollen stinger scorpion and widened that lineage's known evolutionary range.

00:02:10.498 --> 00:02:14.078
Peptides already isolated from scorpion venom have shown activity against

00:02:14.078 --> 00:02:18.278
staphlococcus and drug-resistant tuberculosis strains in lab and animal

00:02:18.278 --> 00:02:24.130
tests. Most people assume a scorpion's venom gland stays sterile because an infected

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:28.790
gland would harm the animal that produced it. That belief treated the gland as a sealed

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:34.410
toxin factory. The fluid those microbes occupy is loaded with protein and harsh in its salt

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:40.310
and ion chemistry. A mix long treated as too hostile for life. This is the first time venom

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:45.810
from these animals has been sequenced from microbiota in a systematic way. That sweep pulled up a

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:50.030
bacteria lineage once treated as rare and now recovered across further species.

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:55.590
The gland is not only packaging toxins for injection, it is also maintaining a filtered

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:58.930
habitat that lets some bacteria persist and keeps others out.

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:03.670
An infection after a sting may therefore start with bacteria that already lived in the

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:07.010
venom, not only with microbes picked up from skin or air.

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:12.570
Follow-up tests can ask whether those resident microbes themselves make compounds with antibiotic

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:13.310
activity.

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:20.914
A 13-protein panel plus clinical variables predicted in a nature metabolism study, insulin sensitivity

00:03:20.914 --> 00:03:28.374
measured by hyperinsulinemic uGlycemic clamp with an R-squared of 0.67 in 161 people.

00:03:28.954 --> 00:03:34.534
In roughly 20,000 UK biobank participants, that model outperformed the triglyceride

00:03:34.534 --> 00:03:38.334
to high-density lipoprotein ratio for type 2 diabetes prediction.

00:03:38.334 --> 00:03:44.894
It also forecasted hepatic, renal, cardiovascular, and respiratory disease in cases where hemoglobin

00:03:44.894 --> 00:03:46.074
A1C did not.

00:03:46.674 --> 00:03:51.354
The proteins reflect contributions from liver, adipose tissue, and immune cells.

00:03:51.914 --> 00:03:56.534
A subset of the signature shifted toward an insulin-sensitive profile after exercise.

00:03:56.934 --> 00:04:01.634
The signature was validated in an independent cohort of 1,700 people.

00:04:02.497 --> 00:04:04.937
University of California researchers flooded.

00:04:04.937 --> 00:04:10.737
As Fizz.org describes, pasture plots at the Sierra Foothill Research and Extension Center

00:04:10.737 --> 00:04:14.317
to measure how irrigation affects rock weathering rates and soils.

00:04:14.957 --> 00:04:19.337
Water moved through different soil depths and down-slope paths over three-hour periods.

00:04:19.937 --> 00:04:24.477
The work is meant to speed up natural mineral reactions that already bind atmospheric carbon

00:04:24.477 --> 00:04:25.037
dioxide.

00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:31.299
Nanotechnology now yields ultra-thin components that control light at scales below its wavelength.

00:04:31.979 --> 00:04:38.239
Researchers outline how those engineered materials can replace bulk lenses and mirrors in imaging and sensing devices.

00:04:39.303 --> 00:04:42.973
Artificial intelligence tools may handle routine screening of research proposals,

00:04:43.843 --> 00:04:47.003
freeing human reviewers to focus on complex ethical judgments.

00:04:47.603 --> 00:04:51.523
That suggestion comes from analysis of current ethics review bottlenecks.

00:04:52.494 --> 00:04:58.354
Researchers in Japan showed that flipping atom-thin layers reverses the direction of heat flow in multi-layer stacks.

00:04:58.354 --> 00:05:02.854
The method is aimed at thermal loads in future three-dimensional computer chips.

00:05:03.534 --> 00:05:09.514
Leipzig University researchers conclude that the human drive to be seen as morally good appears early in development.

00:05:10.014 --> 00:05:17.014
The finding draws on evolutionary and social identity frameworks published in philosophical transactions of the Royal Society B.

00:05:17.814 --> 00:05:26.654
Adding caps to UCLA nanowiles, which already trap individual cells in their secretions, now allows experiments that open-wall designs could not support,

00:05:26.654 --> 00:05:30.334
including work on cancer, heart disease, and protein release.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:35.180
UC Davis researchers are evaluating solar panels over tomato crops

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:37.220
to reduce heat stress and water demand.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:42.980
Early observations show the panels alter the microclimate around the plants during peak summer heat.

00:05:43.889 --> 00:05:47.769
Chest X rays in the United States, more than 70 million of them each year,

00:05:48.249 --> 00:05:53.049
currently report no osteoporotic fracture risk in an NEJMAI analysis

00:05:53.049 --> 00:05:54.989
of what those images already contain.

00:05:54.989 --> 00:06:01.889
New models demonstrate that the existing films hold usable bone density signals that could trigger earlier intervention.

00:06:02.804 --> 00:06:07.764
Citrate, glycinate, and chloride forms of magnesium show, in a found-my-fitness briefing,

00:06:08.264 --> 00:06:12.484
better absorption than oxide, and glycinate is often chosen for evening use.

00:06:13.204 --> 00:06:17.664
Leafy greens, nuts, seeds, and legumes remain the primary recommended sources.

00:06:17.664 --> 00:06:25.544
The adult supplemental upper limit is 350mg of elemental magnesium per day to avoid gastrointestinal effects.

00:06:26.164 --> 00:06:30.444
Athletes and sauna users may need modestly higher intakes because of sweat losses.

